From 354d8d0401df115bce40d7c67c3477644b8f047a Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Hades <15710513+hadesdevls@users.noreply.github.com>
Date: Tue, 17 Nov 2026 20:54:22 +0000
Subject: [PATCH] volume_id: refuse a buffer whose offset and length wrap
 around

volume_id_get_buffer() takes a 64-bit offset that the filesystem
probes compute from fields of the medium. If off + len wraps around,
the size check against known_size passes, and so does the check that
the area fits in the superblock buffer; small_off then keeps the low
bits of the huge offset and the function returns a pointer far outside
id->sbbuf (or reads a wrong area into the seek buffer). A crafted
partition can then make blkid, mount LABEL=/UUID= or findfs read out
of bounds. This is CVE-2026-124746 (CNA Red Hat, 2026-11-11), for which
there is no BusyBox fix yet.

Return NULL, as for any area that cannot be read, when off + len
overflows.
---
 util-linux/volume_id/util.c | 5 +++++
 1 file changed, 5 insertions(+)

diff --git a/util-linux/volume_id/util.c b/util-linux/volume_id/util.c
index b59aa99..2e96aab 100644
--- a/util-linux/volume_id/util.c
+++ b/util-linux/volume_id/util.c
@@ -176,6 +176,11 @@ void *volume_id_get_buffer(struct volume_id *id, uint64_t off, size_t len)
 	unsigned small_off;
 	ssize_t read_len;
 
+	/* The probes add offsets read from the medium: one that wraps
+	 * around would pass the checks below and point outside the buffers */
+	if (off + len < off)
+		return NULL;
+
 	if (id->known_size != UNKNOWN_SIZE
 	 && id->known_size < off + len
 	) {
